WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.267
These words are a heading in Software Engineering

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:04.533
four oh one. Yet a reader who searched

00:00:04.533 --> 00:00:06.800
for that heading could miss the course entirely.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.629
Here is the old search, replayed from the

00:00:09.629 --> 00:00:12.457
published revision. It returns results. Look for the

00:00:12.457 --> 00:00:15.286
course itself. It's missing. A test that only

00:00:15.286 --> 00:00:16.700
counts results would pass.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.318
Save two things. The exact words the reader

00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:21.936
typed, and where the reader needed to land.

00:00:21.936 --> 00:00:24.555
Search is broken is hard to replay. This

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:27.173
little card tells another person exactly what to

00:00:27.173 --> 00:00:27.500
try.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.755
Now use those same words on the corrected

00:00:29.755 --> 00:00:32.009
site. There's the course. Open it. We still

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.700
have one more thing to check.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.189
There is the heading we started with. Tomorrow

00:00:36.189 --> 00:00:38.678
the result might move down a place and

00:00:38.678 --> 00:00:41.167
still be perfectly useful. Test the outcome the

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.100
reader cares about.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.577
After the next search rebuild, run that same

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:47.055
case again. Think of the last thing you

00:00:47.055 --> 00:00:49.532
had to correct twice. What example could you

00:00:49.532 --> 00:00:51.700
save today to catch it next time?
